          equipment: '',
          dealing: 'Deal 6 cards to each player. Turn one card up next to remaining deck (stock) to determine trump suit.',
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: 'Win the hand by scoring the most points through melds and capturing Aces and Tens in tricks (brisques). Win the game by being first to make 500+ points across hands.',
          gameplay: 'Non-dealer leads to first trick. Opponent may play any card (no obligation to follow suit). Higher card of led suit wins, or trump wins against non-trump lead. Winner of trick may declare melds, then both players draw from stock (winner draws first). Winner leads to next trick.',
          keyRules: [
            'Non-dealer leads to first trick; no obligation to follow suit during stock phase.',
            'Higher card of led suit wins the trick, or any trump beats non-trump.',
            'Winner of each trick may declare melds (e.g. marriage, Marjolet with Queen), then winner draws first from stock, opponent second.',
            'First to 500 or more points wins the game; points from melds and from Aces and Tens (brisques) in tricks.',
            'In the final 6 tricks, must follow suit and win if possible.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: 'Balance between playing cards to tricks and keeping cards for melds. Ideally, play low cards to tricks while keeping meldable combinations in hand. Win tricks when you need to declare melds.',
          intermediate: 'The tension between capturing Aces/Tens in tricks vs. using them in melds. Sometimes discard a high card early to develop a meld. The Marjolet (trump Jack) is extremely valuable - it can be melded multiple times with different Queens.',
          advanced: "Memory is essential - track which cards have been played to know what melds are possible. Decide when to 'close' by drawing the last stock card based on your hand strength. In the final 6 tricks, carefully manage your cards to follow the stricter rules.",
          tips: [
            'Be flexible in which melds you chase - adapt based on what cards you draw',
            'The trump Jack (Marjolet) is uniquely powerful because it can be remelded',
            'Consider the timing of melding - showing cards gives opponent information',
            'In the final 6 tricks, planning is crucial - you must follow suit and win if possible',
            "The Dix (7 of trumps) is always worth 10 points - don't forget to claim it",
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
